Continual Learning: To continuously acquire new knowledge while retaining previously learned knowledge and minimizing catastrophic forgetting.
Incremental Learning: To update a model with new data, classes, or information without requiring complete retraining.
Lifelong Learning: To accumulate, refine, and transfer knowledge throughout a sequence of learning experiences.
Class-Incremental Learning: To learn newly introduced classes while maintaining recognition performance on previously learned classes.
Task-Incremental Learning: To sequentially learn multiple tasks while preserving knowledge from earlier tasks.
Domain-Incremental Learning: To adapt to changing data distributions while solving the same underlying task.
Online Learning: To learn and update model parameters continuously as new data arrives.
Experience Replay Learning: To mitigate catastrophic forgetting by revisiting representative samples from previous learning stages.
Memory-Augmented Learning: To enhance learning and knowledge retention through external or internal memory mechanisms.
Knowledge Distillation: To transfer knowledge from one model to another while preserving learned representations and decision-making capabilities.
Graph Continual Learning: To continuously learn from evolving graph structures without losing previously acquired graph knowledge.
Incremental Graph Learning: To incorporate newly added nodes, edges, and graph patterns without rebuilding the entire graph model.
Open-World Learning: To recognize known categories while detecting and adapting to previously unseen categories.
Concept Drift Learning: To identify and adapt to changes in the statistical properties of incoming data over time.
Continual Federated Learning: To continuously learn from decentralized data sources while preserving privacy and previous knowledge.
Transfer Learning: To leverage knowledge learned from one domain or task to improve learning in another.
Meta-Learning: To learn general learning strategies that enable rapid adaptation to new tasks.
Curriculum Learning: To improve learning efficiency by organizing training samples from simpler concepts to more complex concepts.
Active Learning: To maximize learning performance by selecting the most informative samples for labeling.
Self-Supervised Learning: To learn meaningful feature representations from unlabeled data through automatically generated supervision signals.
Semi-Supervised Learning: To exploit both labeled and unlabeled data for improved learning performance.
Few-Shot Learning: To generalize effectively from a very limited number of labeled examples.
Zero-Shot Learning: To recognize unseen classes using semantic relationships and prior knowledge.
Multi-Task Learning: To improve learning by jointly optimizing multiple related tasks.
Federated Learning: To train models collaboratively across distributed data sources without sharing raw data.
Adaptive Learning: To dynamically adjust model behavior according to environmental or data changes.
Reinforcement Learning: To learn optimal decision-making policies through reward-driven interactions with an environment.
Deep Reinforcement Learning: To combine deep representation learning with sequential decision-making capabilities.
Evolutionary Learning: To optimize model parameters or structures using evolutionary search and selection mechanisms.
Representation Learning: To automatically discover informative and compact feature representations from raw data.
Contrastive Learning: To learn discriminative representations by maximizing similarity among related samples and minimizing similarity among unrelated samples.
Dynamic Architecture Learning: To adapt the model structure as learning requirements evolve.
Prototype-Based Learning: To represent classes using prototypical examples for efficient classification and generalization.
Self-Evolving Learning: To autonomously adapt model knowledge and behavior without extensive external intervention.
Streaming Learning: To process and learn from continuous data streams in real time.
Open-Set Recognition Learning: To distinguish between known and unknown classes during inference.
Neuro-Symbolic Learning: To integrate neural learning with symbolic reasoning for enhanced interpretability and reasoning.
Foundation Model Adaptation: To specialize large pre-trained models for specific downstream tasks.
Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT): To adapt pre-trained models using minimal parameter updates.
Knowledge Retention Learning: To preserve previously acquired knowledge while incorporating new information.
Catastrophic Forgetting Mitigation: To prevent degradation of previously learned knowledge during subsequent learning phases.
Anomaly Detection Learning: To identify observations that significantly deviate from expected patterns.
Novelty Detection Learning: To detect previously unseen patterns that differ from known categories.
Domain Adaptation: To reduce performance degradation caused by differences between source and target domains.
Domain Generalization: To learn representations that remain effective across unseen domains.
Graph Representation Learning: To learn meaningful embeddings that capture structural and relational information within graphs.
Self-Adaptive Learning: To automatically adjust learning strategies according to changing conditions.
Hybrid Learning: To combine multiple learning paradigms for improved learning effectiveness.
Explainable Learning (XAI-Based Learning): To improve the transparency and interpretability of model predictions and decisions.
Progressive Learning: To gradually expand knowledge and capabilities while maintaining previously acquired competencies.
